// generated by site/build.mjs: do not edit
STRATA["docs"] = {"learn-strata":{"title":"Learn Strata in Y minutes","html":"<p>Strata is an experimental language for working with agents: constraints live in the types, contracts are proven by an SMT solver (or fuzzed when they can't be), unfinished code is a typed hole, and side effects are capabilities passed as parameters. Its syntax is close to Rust's, with Python's integers and f-strings.</p>\n<p>This page is meant to be read in one go, by a person or an agent, before writing Strata. The code below is a single valid program, exactly as <code>strata render</code> prints it: a test stores it, checks it and runs its tests, so it can't drift from the language. To try it, save it as text in <code>learn.st</code>, store it with <code>strata --file learn.strata edit &lt; learn.st</code>, then run <code>strata --file learn.strata verify</code>.</p>\n<p>For more depth: <a href=\"../docs/language.md\">the language</a>, <a href=\"docs.html?d=verification\">verification</a>, <a href=\"docs.html?d=tools\">tools</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">// A comment belongs to what follows it; /// doc comments describe the next\n// definition, field or variant (the viewer and --help show them).\n\n// ─── 1. Values and expressions ─────────────────────────────────────────────\nfn basics() -&gt; Int {\n  // `x := e` names a value, its type inferred; `let x: T = e` gives the type.\n  // Both are immutable (`mut x := e` and `mut x: T = e` are not).\n  n := 42\n  let x: Float = 3.5\n  ok := true &amp;&amp; !false || n == 42\n  s := \"hello\"\n\n  // Int never overflows (it grows into a big integer). Float is never NaN.\n  // No implicit Int/Float mixing: convert with float(n), round(x), floor, ceil.\n  mixed := float(n) * x\n  back := round(mixed)\n\n  // f-strings interpolate any expression; specs after `:` as in Python.\n  msg := f\"{s}, {n} items, {x:.2}, {n:&gt;5}, {{literal braces}}\"\n\n  // `+` adds numbers and joins strings and lists.\n  greeting := s + \" world\"\n  both := [1, 2] + [3]\n\n  // Everything is an expression: `if` and blocks have values.\n  sign := if n &gt; 0 {\n    \"+\"\n  } else if n &lt; 0 {\n    \"-\"\n  } else {\n    \"0\"\n  }\n  y := {\n    a := 1\n    a + 1\n  }\n  back + y + len(greeting) + len(both) + len(msg) + len(sign)\n}\n\n// ─── 2. Functions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// Parameters and the result are always typed. The last expression is the result.\nfn add(a: Int, b: Int) -&gt; Int {\n  a + b\n}\n\n// `()` is the unit type: \"no meaningful value\".\nfn shout(s: String) {\n  print(to_upper(s))\n}\n\n// Lambdas: `x -&gt; x + 1`, `(a, b) -&gt; a + b`, `() -&gt; 7`, `x -&gt; { ... }`.\n// Method-call syntax works on any function: xs.map(f) is map(xs, f).\nfn evens_squared(xs: List[Int]) -&gt; List[Int] {\n  xs.filter(x -&gt; x % 2 == 0).map(x -&gt; x * x)\n}\n\n// A named function can be passed as a value (an overloaded builtin such as\n// `len` needs a lambda: `w -&gt; len(w)`).\nfn line_totals(lines: List[Line]) -&gt; List[Cents] {\n  lines.map(line_total)\n}\n\n// ─── 3. Refinement types: a type plus a condition on `it` ──────────────────\ntype Quantity = Int\n  where 1 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 99\n\ntype Cents = Int\n  where it &gt;= 0\n\ntype Sku = String\n  where matches(it, \"[A-Z]{3}-[0-9]{1,4}\")\n\n// Refinements are structural: any Int may be passed as a Quantity; the check\n// happens at the boundary (compile time for literals, run time otherwise, and\n// proven or refuted by `strata verify`). Inline refinements need no name:\nfn pick(level: Int where 5 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 13) -&gt; Positive {\n  level - 4\n}\n\n// A result's refinement can mention parameters.\nfn halve(n: Natural) -&gt; Int where it &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; it * 2 &lt;= n {\n  n / 2\n}\n\n// The prelude has: Natural, Positive, Negative, NonZero, Probability,\n// Percentage, NonEmpty[T], NonEmptyString, and I8..I64 / U8..U64.\n// `type Name = String` alone is an error: add `where`, or use `distinct`.\n\n// A distinct type is nominal: it never mixes with its base or other types.\ndistinct type UserId = String\n  where matches(it, \"u_[0-9]+\")\n\nfn user_number(id: UserId) -&gt; String {\n  // build one with UserId(\"u_42\"); `.value` is the underlying String\n  id.value[2..]\n}\n\n// ─── 4. Contracts: requires / ensures (`it` is the result) ─────────────────\nfn divide(a: Int, b: Int) -&gt; Int\n  requires b != 0\n  ensures b &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; a &gt;= 0 ==&gt; it &gt;= 0 {\n  a / b\n}\n\n// ─── 5. Records, variants and unions ───────────────────────────────────────\ntype Line = { sku: Sku, qty: Quantity, unit_price: Cents }\n\n// A sum type. Each variant is itself a type (`Shipped` can be a parameter type),\n// so variant names are unique across the whole project.\ntype Order =\n  | Pending { id: NonEmptyString, lines: NonEmpty[Line] }\n  | Shipped { id: NonEmptyString, lines: NonEmpty[Line], tracking: NonEmptyString }\n  | Cancelled { id: NonEmptyString, reason: NonEmptyString }\n\n// Variants without fields make an enum; tuple-style variants exist too.\ntype Size =\n  | Small\n  | Large\n\n// A union of existing types. It normalizes like a set (`Int | Positive` is Int).\ntype Square = { side: Positive }\n\ntype Circle = { radius: Positive }\n\ntype Shape = Square | Circle\n\n// Generic types take parameters in brackets.\ntype Pair[A, B] = { first: A, second: B }\n\n// A field can have a default, after its type; a constructor may leave it out.\ntype Retry = {\n  attempts: Int where 1 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 10 = 3,\n  label: String = \"retry\",\n}\n\nfn records() -&gt; Line {\n  line := Line { sku: \"TEA-7\", qty: 2, unit_price: 450 }\n  qty := line.qty\n  // Functional update: copy with some fields changed.\n  more := Line { ..line, qty: qty + 1 }\n  // Shorthand: `Line { sku, qty, unit_price }` when variables have those names.\n  p := Pair { first: 1, second: \"one\" }\n  t := (more.sku, p.first)\n  // Tuples: destructure with :=, or read t.0, t.1.\n  (code, count) := t\n  r := Retry { label: \"fetch\" }\n  Line { ..more, sku: code, qty: min(count + r.attempts, 99) }\n}\n\n// ─── 6. Pattern matching: exhaustive, and arms narrow ──────────────────────\nfn line_total(l: Line) -&gt; Cents {\n  l.qty * l.unit_price\n}\n\nfn status(o: Order) -&gt; String {\n  match o {\n    Pending { id, .. } =&gt; f\"{id} is pending\",\n    // `o` is narrowed: inside this arm it is a Shipped, so o.tracking works.\n    Shipped =&gt; f\"shipped: {o.tracking}\",\n    Cancelled { reason, .. } =&gt; f\"cancelled: {reason}\",\n  }\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -&gt; Int {\n  match s {\n    Square =&gt; s.side * s.side,\n    Circle =&gt; 3 * s.radius * s.radius,\n  }\n}\n\nfn kind_of(v: Int | String) -&gt; String {\n  match v {\n    Int =&gt; f\"number {v}\",\n    String =&gt; f\"text of {len(v)}\",\n  }\n}\n\n// Patterns: a type name, a variant (with fields), a literal, a condition on\n// `it`, alternatives with `|`, list and tuple shapes, and `_`.\nfn classify(n: Int) -&gt; String {\n  match n {\n    0 =&gt; \"zero\",\n    1 | 2 | 3 =&gt; \"small\",\n    where it &lt; 0 =&gt; \"negative\",\n    where 4 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 99 =&gt; \"medium\",\n    _ =&gt; \"large\",\n  }\n}\n\n// Lists match by shape; `..rest` binds the remainder.\nfn length(xs: List[Int]) -&gt; Natural {\n  match xs {\n    [] =&gt; 0,\n    [_, ..rest] =&gt; 1 + length(rest),\n  }\n}\n\n// Match on a computed value by naming it with `as`.\nfn first_word(text: String) -&gt; String {\n  match split(text, \" \") as ws {\n    [w, ..] =&gt; w,\n    [] =&gt; \"\",\n  }\n}\n\n// `x is Pattern` tests without matching.\nfn is_open(o: Order) -&gt; Bool {\n  o is Pending\n}\n\n// ─── 7. Errors: Option, Result, `!` `?`, `except` `or`, `!!` `??` ───────────\ntype ParseError =\n  | Blank\n  | NotANumber { text: String }\n\nfn parse_qty(s: String) -&gt; Result[Quantity, ParseError] {\n  if len(trim(s)) == 0 {\n    return Err(Blank)\n  }\n  match parse_int(trim(s)) {\n    Some(n) =&gt; if 1 &lt;= n &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 99 {\n      Ok(n)\n    } else {\n      Err(NotANumber { text: s })\n    },\n    None =&gt; Err(NotANumber { text: s }),\n  }\n}\n\n// e!  : the Ok value, or return the Err from this function.\n// e?  : the Some value, or return None from this function.\nfn double_qty(s: String) -&gt; Result[Int, ParseError] {\n  q := parse_qty(s)!\n  Ok(q * 2)\n}\n\nfn first_upper(words: List[String]) -&gt; Option[String] {\n  Some(to_upper(words.find(w -&gt; len(w) &gt; 0)?))\n}\n\n// Handle it here instead. A Result: `e !! fallback`, or `e except err { … }`.\n// An Option: `e ?? fallback`, or `e or { … }`. A block may `return`.\n// Each pair works on its own type only.\nfn qty_or_one(s: String) -&gt; Int {\n  parse_qty(s) !! 1\n}\n\nfn port(text: String) -&gt; Int {\n  parse_int(text) ?? 8080\n}\n\nfn first_long(words: List[String]) -&gt; String {\n  w := words.find(w -&gt; len(w) &gt; 3) or { return \"none\" }\n  to_upper(w)\n}\n\nfn explain(s: String) -&gt; String {\n  q := parse_qty(s) except e {\n    return match e {\n      Blank =&gt; \"blank\",\n      NotANumber { text } =&gt; f\"not a number: {text}\",\n    }\n  }\n  f\"quantity {q}\"\n}\n\n// ─── 8. Mutation: immutable by default, always visible ─────────────────────\nfn total(lines: List[Line]) -&gt; Natural {\n  mut sum: Natural = 0\n  for line in lines {\n    sum += line_total(line)\n  }\n  sum\n}\n\n// A `mut` parameter changes the caller's variable; the call says `mut` too.\nfn restock(mut line: Line, extra: Natural) {\n  line.qty = min(line.qty + extra, 99)\n}\n\nfn loops() -&gt; Int {\n  mut line := Line { sku: \"CUP-1\", qty: 1, unit_price: 1200 }\n  restock(mut line, 5)\n  mut i := 0\n  while i &lt; 10 {\n    i += 1\n  }\n  loop {\n    i -= 1\n    if i % 2 == 1 {\n      continue\n    }\n    if i &lt; 3 {\n      break\n    }\n  }\n  // `0..3` is 0, 1, 2 and `1..=3` is 1, 2, 3: ranges are lists of Int.\n  for k in 0..3 {\n    i += k\n  }\n  i + line.qty\n}\n\n// No shared references: values are copied, so nothing changes behind your\n// back. A declared type holds for a variable's whole life (every assignment\n// is checked). Closures cannot change outer variables.\n\n// ─── 9. Collections (all immutable: operations return new values) ──────────\nfn collections(xs: NonEmpty[Int], text: String) -&gt; Int {\n  // xs[i] must be in range: checked at run time, proven by `verify` (here xs\n  // is NonEmpty). get(xs, i) gives an Option instead. No negative indices.\n  head := xs[0]\n  maybe := get(xs, 5) ?? 0\n  // Slices take a range and are clamped, as in Python; strings too.\n  rest := xs[1..]\n  prefix := text[..3]\n  // Comprehensions, with `where` to filter.\n  squares := [x * x for x in xs where x &gt; 0]\n  grid := [(a, b) for a in 1..=2 for b in [\"x\", \"y\"]]\n  d := Dict { \"tea\": 450, \"cup\": 1200 }\n  d2 := insert(d, \"pot\", 3000)\n  price := get(d2, \"tea\") ?? 0\n  lengths := Dict { w: len(w) for w in split(text, \" \") }\n  s := Set { 1, 2, 2, 3 }\n  empty := Dict[String, Int] {}\n  total := fold(xs, 0, (acc, x) -&gt; acc + x)\n  pairs := enumerate(zip(sort(xs), [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]))\n  head\n    + maybe\n    + len(rest)\n    + len(prefix)\n    + len(squares)\n    + len(grid)\n    + price\n    + len(lengths)\n    + len(s)\n    + len(empty)\n    + total\n    + len(pairs)\n}\n\n// ─── 10. Traits: what a type can do ────────────────────────────────────────\ntrait Priced {\n  fn price(self) -&gt; Natural\n}\n\ntrait Describe {\n  // A contract on a trait method binds every implementation.\n  fn describe(self) -&gt; String\n    ensures len(it) &gt; 0\n}\n\n// Supertraits, default methods, and laws (checked by `strata verify`).\ntrait Summary: Describe + Priced {\n  fn summary(self) -&gt; String {\n    f\"{self.describe()}: {self.price()}c\"\n  }\n}\n\ntrait Combine {\n  fn combine(self, other: Self) -&gt; Self\n  law associative(a: Self, b: Self, c: Self):\n    a.combine(b).combine(c) == a.combine(b.combine(c))\n}\n\n// A method without `self` is called on a type: Money.empty(), T.empty().\ntrait Empty: Combine {\n  fn empty() -&gt; Self\n  law identity(a: Self): Self.empty().combine(a) == a\n}\n\ntype Item = { name: NonEmptyString, cents: Natural }\n\nimpl Priced for Item {\n  fn price(self) -&gt; Natural {\n    self.cents\n  }\n}\n\nimpl Describe for Item {\n  fn describe(self) -&gt; String {\n    self.name\n  }\n}\n\n// Every method has a default here, so the block is empty.\nimpl Summary for Item {}\n\n// A type's own methods (no trait), in the module that defines the type.\nimpl Item {\n  fn new(name: NonEmptyString) -&gt; Item {\n    Item { name, cents: 0 }\n  }\n\n  fn priced(self, cents: Natural) -&gt; Item {\n    Item { ..self, cents }\n  }\n}\n\ntype Money = { cents: Int }\n\nimpl Combine for Money {\n  fn combine(self, other: Self) -&gt; Self {\n    Money { cents: self.cents + other.cents }\n  }\n}\n\nimpl Empty for Money {\n  fn empty() -&gt; Money {\n    Money { cents: 0 }\n  }\n}\n\n// Generic impls, also for built-in types.\nimpl[T] Combine for List[T] {\n  fn combine(self, other: Self) -&gt; Self {\n    self + other\n  }\n}\n\n// Generic functions state their bounds.\nfn combine_all[T: Empty](xs: List[T]) -&gt; T {\n  fold(xs, T.empty(), (acc, x) -&gt; acc.combine(x))\n}\n\n// A trait used as a type means \"any implementation\": this is\n// `fn receipt[T: Summary](x: T)`. Name the parameter only to tie things together.\nfn receipt(x: Summary) -&gt; String {\n  x.summary()\n}\n\n// ─── 11. Capabilities are traits passed as parameters ──────────────────────\n// No hidden effects: a database or clock is a trait, so the signature shows\n// what code can touch. A method that changes its value takes `mut self`, and\n// its calls are marked: `mut db.save(o)`.\ntrait Db {\n  fn load(self, id: NonEmptyString) -&gt; Option[Order]\n  fn save(mut self, o: Order)\n}\n\ntype ShipError =\n  | NotFound\n  | NotPending\n\nfn ship(\n  mut db: Db,\n  id: NonEmptyString,\n  tracking: NonEmptyString,\n) -&gt; Result[Order, ShipError]\n  ensures match it {\n    Ok(o) =&gt; o is Shipped,\n    Err(_) =&gt; true,\n  } {\n  order := db.load(id) or { return Err(NotFound) }\n  match order {\n    Pending { id, lines } =&gt; {\n      o := Shipped { id, lines, tracking }\n      mut db.save(o)\n      Ok(o)\n    },\n    _ =&gt; Err(NotPending),\n  }\n}\n\n// ─── 12. Typed holes: unfinished code that still checks ────────────────────\n// `?name` stands for an expression of the expected type. `strata holes` lists\n// each with its goal type, context and obligations: a task for an agent.\nfn refund(o: Order) -&gt; Cents\n  ensures it &gt;= 0\n  = ?refund_policy\n\n// ─── 13. Termination ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// `verify` shows termination itself (a decreasing measure) or says\n// \"may not terminate\". `terminates` after the contract assumes it.\nfn collatz(n: Positive) -&gt; Natural\n  terminates {\n  if n == 1 {\n    0\n  } else if n % 2 == 0 {\n    1 + collatz(n / 2)\n  } else {\n    1 + collatz(3 * n + 1)\n  }\n}\n\n// ─── 14. Tests ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ntest \"orders\" {\n  l := Line { sku: \"TEA-7\", qty: 2, unit_price: 450 }\n  assert line_total(l) == 900\n  assert classify(-5) == \"negative\"\n  assert explain(\"x\") == \"not a number: x\"\n  assert combine_all([Money { cents: 1 }, Money { cents: 2 }]) == Money { cents: 3 }\n  assert Item.new(\"tea\").priced(42).summary() == \"tea: 42c\"\n  assert receipt(Item.new(\"cup\")) == \"cup: 0c\"\n}\n\ntest \"a mocked capability\" {\n  // `mock Db` is an implementation whose methods return arbitrary valid values.\n  mut db := mock Db\n  r := ship(mut db, \"ord_1\", \"TRK9\")\n  assert r is Ok || r is Err\n}</pre>\n<h2 id=\"rules-that-catch-newcomers\">Rules that catch newcomers</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Never write a <code>.strata</code> file yourself.</strong> It is a binary module file: the syntax tree. Code goes in as text through an edit (<code>strata edit</code>, <code>strata add</code>, or <code>strata --file F edit &lt; text</code> outside a package), which parses it, checks it and stores the tree; it comes out as text through <code>strata render</code> / <code>strata view</code>. How you lay the text out doesn't matter: it is printed back in one canonical style, the one the code above is in.</li><li><strong>Every name a variant introduces is a type</strong>, so it must be unique in its module: no other variant, type or trait of that module may share it. Another module's type of the same name is told apart by its module (<code>geo.Circle</code>).</li><li><strong>In one module, a function can't share a name with a trait method</strong> (<code>fn describe</code> next to <code>trait Describe { fn describe … }</code>), since <code>x.describe()</code> could mean either. Across modules it can: <code>x.describe()</code> is the method, <code>describe(x)</code> the function.</li><li><strong>Overloaded builtins can't be passed by name</strong> (<code>len</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>sum</code>…): write <code>xs.map(x -&gt; len(x))</code>. Ordinary functions can: <code>lines.map(line_total)</code>. Methods whose meaning depends on the receiver also need a lambda: <code>xs.map(x -&gt; x.describe())</code>.</li><li><strong><code>type Name = String</code> is an error</strong>: a bare alias adds nothing. Add a <code>where</code>, or say <code>distinct type</code>.</li><li><strong>Any function can be called as a method</strong> on its first argument: <code>xs.filter(p)</code> is <code>filter(xs, p)</code>, <code>line.line_total()</code> is <code>line_total(line)</code>.</li><li><strong><code>mut</code> is visible on both sides</strong>: <code>fn f(mut x: T)</code> and the call <code>f(mut x)</code>; <code>fn save(mut self, …)</code> and the call <code>mut db.save(o)</code> (required there, an error anywhere else). Only a <code>mut</code> variable, a <code>mut</code> parameter or a field of one can be changed, and a <code>mut</code> argument needs exactly the parameter's type. As an argument, a marked call takes parentheses: <code>f((mut q.pop()), x)</code>.</li><li><strong>A declared type holds forever</strong>: <code>mut x: Positive = 1</code> then <code>x = 0</code> is an error (at compile time for literals, at run time otherwise).</li><li><strong>Matches are exhaustive and every arm must be reachable.</strong> Conditions comparing <code>it</code> with constants are decided exactly; anything else (<code>matches(it, …)</code>, a call) needs a <code>_</code> arm.</li><li><strong><code>Result</code> and <code>Option</code> each have their own operators</strong>: <code>!</code>, <code>except</code>, <code>!!</code> for a <code>Result</code>; <code>?</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>??</code> for an <code>Option</code>. Using one on the other type is an error. A single-value fallback is always written short (<code>x ?? 0</code>, not <code>x or { 0 }</code>); <code>??</code> and <code>!!</code> bind looser than arithmetic and tighter than comparisons.</li><li><strong>No implicit conversions</strong>: <code>Int</code> and <code>Float</code> don't mix, and there is no conversion between error types (<code>!</code> passes an error up only when it already fits the function's error type: the same type, one of its variants, or a member of its union).</li><li><strong>Comments attach to what follows them.</strong> A comment where nothing follows (after a block's last statement, inside an argument list) is an error, not silently dropped.</li><li><strong>Empty literals need a type when nothing says it</strong>: <code>let xs: List[Money] = []</code>, <code>Dict[String, Int] {}</code>.</li><li><strong>Indexing is checked</strong>: <code>xs[i]</code> faults when <code>i</code> is out of range, and <code>verify</code> tries to prove it isn't. There are no negative indices (<code>xs[len(xs) - 1]</code>); use <code>get(xs, i)</code> for an <code>Option</code>.</li><li><strong><code>print(x)</code> is for convenience.</strong> Code whose output matters takes a <code>Console</code> (<code>std.io</code>), so tests can record it.</li><li>Not in the language (yet): shared references, exceptions, null, implicit effects, generators, async, macros, inheritance.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"modules-and-packages\">Modules and packages</h2>\n<p>A file is a module, named after its path in the package (<code>shop/orders.strata</code> is <code>shop.orders</code>; <code>main.strata</code> is the root module). A folder with a <code>strata.toml</code> is a package (<code>strata init</code>); a lone <code>.strata</code> file is a script. Definitions are private unless <code>pub</code>, and modules meet only through their public contract (<code>pub</code> types, signatures, contracts, laws).</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">// shop/orders.strata\npub type Order = { id: OrderId, lines: NonEmpty[Line] }\n\npub fn ship(mut db: Db, id: OrderId) -&gt; Result[Order, ShipError] { ... }\n\nfn helper() -&gt; Int { ... }             // private to shop.orders\n\n// billing.strata\nuse shop.orders.{Order, ship}          // or `use shop.orders.*`, or `shop.orders.ship(x)`</pre>\n<h2 id=\"programs\">Programs</h2>\n<p><code>main</code> asks for what it needs by type and <code>strata run</code> passes it in: a command-line options record, <code>Console</code> (<code>std.io</code>, chosen by the environment), and capabilities it names: <code>Disk</code> (<code>std.fs</code>, a <code>Files</code>), <code>Process</code> (<code>std.process</code>, an <code>Environment</code>), or the program's own. It returns <code>()</code>, an <code>ExitCode</code>, or a <code>Result</code> of either (an <code>Err</code> exits with 1).</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">use std.cli.CommandLine\nuse std.io.Console\n\n/// Greet people by name.\ntype Options = {\n  /// Shout the greeting.\n  loud: Bool = false,                              // a flag: --loud\n  /// Say it this many times.\n  times: Int where 1 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 10 = 1,        // an option, as it has a default: --times 3\n  /// Who to greet.\n  names: List[String] = [],                        // the positional arguments\n}\n\nimpl CommandLine for Options {}                    // --help comes from the /// comments\n\nfn main(opts: Options, mut console: Console) {\n  for name in opts.names {\n    for _ in 0..opts.times {\n      mut console.print(if opts.loud { to_upper(f\"hello, {name}!\") } else { f\"hello, {name}!\" })\n    }\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p>Tests pass stand-ins instead of the real capabilities: <code>recorder(input)</code> / <code>printed(r)</code> for a <code>Console</code>, <code>mem_files([(path, text)])</code> for <code>Files</code>, <code>fake_environment(args, vars)</code>, or <code>mock Trait</code>. Nothing but <code>strata run</code> and <code>strata serve</code> reaches the real terminal, disk or network.</p>\n<p>The standard library is written in Strata and needs no dependency: <code>std.io</code>, <code>std.fs</code>, <code>std.process</code>, <code>std.cli</code>, <code>std.http</code> (<code>Request</code>, <code>Response</code>, <code>Router</code>: <code>app.get(\"/users/:id\", handler)</code>, <code>req.param(\"id\")</code>) and <code>std.web</code> (<code>Html[M]</code> as values: <code>div([class(\"x\")], [text(\"hi\")])</code>, <code>on_click(msg)</code>). A web server defines <code>fn handle(req: Request) -&gt; Response</code>; an app defines <code>init() -&gt; Model</code>, <code>update(model, msg) -&gt; Model</code> and <code>view(model) -&gt; Html[Msg]</code> (the Elm architecture). Both run with <code>strata serve</code>. See <code>examples/hello</code>, <code>wc</code>, <code>page</code>, <code>counter</code>, <code>todos</code>. <code>strata builtins</code> lists every builtin with its signature.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-tools\">The tools</h2>\n<p>Every command works on the package around the current folder, or on a file or folder with <code>--file PATH</code>. Code is stored as binary <code>.strata</code> module files, so you read it through the views and change it through edits; <code>strata render --split DIR</code> writes a package out as <code>.st</code> text files, for reading only.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sh\">strata check                  # types, exhaustiveness, refinements of literals. Run it after every edit\nstrata test [FILTER]          # run `test \"…\" { … }` blocks\nstrata verify [NAME]          # prove contracts with Z3/cvc5, fuzz the rest, show termination\nstrata verify NAME --checks   # …listing every check and how it was settled\nstrata holes                  # each ?hole: goal type, what's in scope, what it must satisfy\nstrata run [PATH] -- ARGS     # run main\nstrata run FN '[JSON args]'   # call one function; args are checked against the types (--trace)\nstrata serve [PATH]           # run `handle` or an app (init/update/view) on http://127.0.0.1:8000\nstrata build [PATH] -o OUT    # native executable through LLVM (part of the language so far)\nstrata builtins               # every builtin and prelude type</pre>\n<p><code>verify</code> gives each function one result: <code>✓</code> proven, <code>~</code> fuzzed (some checks unproven, no failure found; each says why), <code>✗</code> failed (with a counterexample that was run to confirm it), <code>○</code> incomplete (a hole), <code>≈</code> assumed. Proven doesn't imply it terminates: that is reported on its own line.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading and editing a package.</strong> Code is stored as one canonical file per module, but the tools work on definitions:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sh\">strata view --level 0                 # a map: one line per definition, deps, holes, status\nstrata view slice:Order --level 1     # Order, what it uses, what uses it: interfaces only\nstrata view 'ship,refund' --level 2   # full source of a few definitions (levels: 0 map, 1 interfaces, 2 source)\nstrata interface shop.orders          # a module's public contract\nstrata edit [MODULE] -m \"why\" &lt; defs.strata\n                                      # add or replace definitions by name; refused unless the\n                                      # whole package still checks. --rm NAME deletes one\nstrata rename OLD NEW                 # functions, types, variants, trait methods, fields (Type.field)\nstrata move NAME MODULE               # to another module, imports follow\nstrata log / strata diff              # the change history, as structural diffs\nstrata ui                             # live viewer in the browser (http://127.0.0.1:7777)</pre>\n<p>An <code>edit</code> that breaks code elsewhere turns each broken use into a hole (<code>?pending_1</code>, …) and opens a pending change: fill them with more <code>edit</code>s, then <code>strata done</code> (or <code>strata abort</code>). In a git repository every recorded change is also a commit.</p>\n<p><strong>Before pushing:</strong> <code>strata ci</code> (everything checks, exports exist, every module file is exactly as strata writes it; <code>--fix</code> rewrites them). <code>strata setup-git</code> makes <code>git diff</code> show <code>.strata</code> files as text and merge them definition by definition. <code>strata sync</code> merges <code>main</code> into the branch definition by definition, applies the other side's renames, and commits only if <code>ci</code> passes.</p>\n<p><strong>A good loop for an agent:</strong> read with <code>view --level 1</code> and <code>holes</code>; write definitions; <code>check</code>; <code>test</code>; <code>verify</code> the functions you touched, and when one fails, read the counterexample, then fix the code or strengthen the contract (<code>requires</code>, a refinement) until it is proven.</p>","toc":[{"n":2,"id":"rules-that-catch-newcomers","text":"Rules that catch newcomers"},{"n":2,"id":"modules-and-packages","text":"Modules and packages"},{"n":2,"id":"programs","text":"Programs"},{"n":2,"id":"the-tools","text":"The tools"}]},"verification":{"title":"Verification","html":"<p><code>strata verify</code> proves a program's contracts with an SMT solver, runs the solver's counterexamples to confirm them, and fuzzes whatever it can't prove. It also says whether each function terminates. The examples below come from <a href=\"../docs/../examples/math.strata\"><code>examples/math.strata</code></a>; under each is what <code>strata --file examples/math.strata verify --checks</code> says about it.</p>\n<p>Each function gets one of these results:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>result</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>✓</code></td><td>proven</td><td>the solver proved every check</td></tr><tr><td><code>~</code></td><td>fuzzed</td><td>some checks couldn't be proven; generated inputs found no failure, and each unproven check says why</td></tr><tr><td><code>✗</code></td><td>failed</td><td>a counterexample, from the solver (confirmed by running it) or from the fuzzer</td></tr><tr><td><code>○</code></td><td>incomplete</td><td>the function still has a hole</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"refinements\">Refinements</h2>\n<p>A refinement is a type with a constraint. Every place a value must satisfy one (a result, an argument, a field, an assignment) is a check.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">type Digit = Int where 0 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 9\n\n/// Wrong: a digit plus one isn't always a digit.\nfn next_digit(d: Digit) -&gt; Digit {\n  d + 1\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✗ next_digit               Refinement violation (found by the solver)\n    input:   [9]\n    result of `next_digit`: 10 does not satisfy `Digit` (where 0 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 9)</pre>\n<p>The solver found <code>d = 9</code>, and running <code>next_digit(9)</code> confirmed it: a counterexample is only reported once it really fails.</p>\n<h2 id=\"recursion-and-termination\">Recursion and termination</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">/// n! is never 0.\nfn factorial(n: Natural) -&gt; Positive {\n  if n == 0 { 1 } else { n * factorial(n - 1) }\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ factorial                proven (2 checks)\n    proven:  argument `n` of `factorial`: Natural\n    proven:  result of `factorial`: Positive\n    terminates: `n` goes down on every recursive call and is never negative there</pre>\n<p>The recursive call is checked against <code>factorial</code>'s own contract: <code>n - 1</code> is a <code>Natural</code> there (since <code>n != 0</code>), and the result is <code>Positive</code> because <code>n</code> and the callee's result are. Termination needs a <em>measure</em>, a number that goes down on every recursive call and is never negative. The verifier finds it on its own, trying each integer parameter, each list's length, and the differences in the function's comparisons:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn gcd(a: Natural, b: Natural) -&gt; Natural\n  ensures a &gt; 0 || b &gt; 0 ==&gt; it &gt; 0 {\n  if b == 0 { a } else { gcd(b, a % b) }\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ gcd                      proven (5 checks)\n    proven:  no division by zero\n    proven:  argument `a` of `gcd`: Natural\n    proven:  argument `b` of `gcd`: Natural\n    proven:  result of `gcd`: Natural\n    proven:  ensures a &gt; 0 || b &gt; 0 ==&gt; it &gt; 0\n    terminates: `b` goes down on every recursive call and is never negative there</pre>\n<p>When no single number goes down, a pair compared in order may, as for Ackermann's function:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn ackermann(m: Natural, n: Natural) -&gt; Positive {\n  if m == 0 {\n    n + 1\n  } else {\n    if n == 0 { ackermann(m - 1, 1) } else { ackermann(m - 1, ackermann(m, n - 1)) }\n  }\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ ackermann                proven (7 checks)\n    terminates: the pair (`m`, `n`), compared in order, goes down on every recursive call and is never negative there</pre>\n<h2 id=\"loops\">Loops</h2>\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop over a list always ends. Here every assignment must keep <code>total</code> a <code>Natural</code>, its declared type:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn triangle(n: Natural) -&gt; Natural {\n  mut total: Natural = 0\n  for i in 1..=n {\n    total += i\n  }\n  total\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ triangle                 proven (3 checks)\n    proven:  `let` annotated `Natural`\n    proven:  assigning `total`: Natural\n    proven:  result of `triangle`: Natural\n    terminates: no recursion, and only `for` loops</pre>\n<p>A <code>while</code> loop ends when a bound taken from its condition goes down on every pass. A property that holds <em>after</em> a loop usually needs a loop invariant, which the verifier doesn't infer yet: then the check is fuzzed, and the result says so.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn isqrt(n: Natural) -&gt; Natural\n  ensures it * it &lt;= n {\n  mut r: Natural = 0\n  while (r + 1) * (r + 1) &lt;= n {\n    r += 1\n  }\n  r\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">~ isqrt                    fuzzed: 200 cases, 0 discarded by `requires`; 3 of 4 checks proven\n    not proven: ensures it * it &lt;= n: the solver found a possible counterexample: n = 0, but running it did not fail\n    terminates: `while (r + 1) * (r + 1) &lt;= n` ends: `n - (r + 1) * (r + 1) + 1` goes down on every pass</pre>\n<p>Termination is never assumed. Whether the Collatz sequence always reaches 1 is an open problem, so:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn collatz_steps(n: Positive) -&gt; Natural {\n  mut x: Positive = n\n  mut steps: Natural = 0\n  while x != 1 {\n    if x % 2 == 0 { x /= 2 } else { x = 3 * x + 1 }\n    steps += 1\n  }\n  steps\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ collatz_steps            proven (6 checks)\n    may not terminate: the loop `while x != 1`: no bound was shown to go down (tried `x - 1`, `1 - x`)</pre>\n<p>Its checks are proven (every assignment keeps <code>x</code> a <code>Positive</code>); only termination is unknown. A function can be marked <code>terminates</code> to assume it, which is reported as assumed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lists\">Lists</h2>\n<p>A list is known by its length and by how its elements are made, so \"every element satisfies P\" can be proven:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn positive_squares(xs: List[Int]) -&gt; List[Positive] {\n  [x * x for x in xs where x &gt; 0]\n}\n\nfn is_prime(n: Int) -&gt; Bool\n  ensures it ==&gt; n &gt;= 2 {\n  n &gt;= 2 &amp;&amp; all(2..n, d -&gt; n % d != 0)\n}\n\nfn primes(limit: Natural) -&gt; List[Int where it &gt;= 2] {\n  (0..=limit).filter(is_prime)\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ positive_squares         proven (1 check)\n✓ is_prime                 proven (2 checks)\n    proven:  no division by zero\n    proven:  ensures it ==&gt; n &gt;= 2\n✓ primes                   proven (1 check)</pre>\n<p>The comprehension keeps the positives (a <code>filter</code>) and squares them (a <code>map</code>), so they stay positive. In <code>is_prime</code>, every <code>d</code> in <code>2..n</code> is at least 2, so <code>n % d</code> never divides by zero. <code>primes</code> keeps only numbers that passed <code>is_prime</code>, whose contract says they are at least 2. What is known of every element also flows in from a contract:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">fn first_or_one(xs: List[Int]) -&gt; Positive\n  requires all(xs, x -&gt; x &gt; 0) {\n  match xs {\n    [x, ..] =&gt; x,\n    [] =&gt; 1,\n  }\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✓ first_or_one             proven (1 check)</pre>\n<h2 id=\"bugs-the-solver-finds\">Bugs the solver finds</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rust\">/// Wrong: an empty list has no average.\nfn average(xs: List[Int]) -&gt; Int {\n  sum(xs) / len(xs)\n}</pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">✗ average                  Runtime violation (found by the solver)\n    input:   [[]]\n    division by zero (stack: average)</pre>\n<h2 id=\"reference\">Reference</h2>\n<p><code>strata verify</code> turns every contract into proof obligations and gives each function one of these results:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>proven</strong> (<code>✓</code>): the SMT solver proved every check. <code>--checks</code> lists them.</li><li><strong>fuzzed</strong> (<code>~</code>): some checks could not be proven, so the function was run on generated inputs and nothing failed. Each unproven check says why, for example <em>depends on <code>fold</code> is not modelled</em>.</li><li><strong>failed</strong> (<code>✗</code>): a counterexample. When the solver finds one, it is run in the interpreter first, and reported only if it really fails. Otherwise the fuzzer looks for one.</li><li><strong>incomplete</strong> (<code>○</code>): the function still has a hole.</li><li><strong>assumed</strong> (<code>≈</code>): trusted, not checked. These are capabilities (<code>capability ...</code>: std's <code>Terminal</code>, <code>Disk</code> and <code>Process</code>, and a program's own) and the code that takes one, which only the runtime can give, and code that reaches C through <code>extern c</code>: they call the system, so <code>verify</code> never runs them. Their contracts are taken as true, and callers are still checked against those contracts as usual.</li></ul>\n<p>Derived code is checked as well: <code>derive Json for Signup</code> makes <code>Signup.encode</code> and the rest, and also copies of <code>std.json</code>'s helpers made for <code>Signup</code>'s fields (<code>std.json.encode_as.for.Signup.4</code>), which are listed and checked with it, so a fault that only some field types can cause is reported there (with <code>integers = \"proven\"</code> under <code>[json]</code>, an <code>Int</code> field that may be beyond what JSON holds exactly).</p>\n<p>The checks are exactly the interpreter's runtime checks: a callee's parameter refinements and <code>requires</code>, the refinements of constructed values and assigned fields, annotated bindings, <code>assert</code>, division by zero, and the function's own result type and <code>ensures</code>. Calls are checked against the callee's contract, never its body, which is how modules meet. A function passed by name (<code>lines.map(line_total)</code>) is checked against one arbitrary element of the list.</p>\n<p>How it works (<code>src/smt.rs</code>): the body is run symbolically, with values as SMT terms. Records and sums become structures of terms, a list is known by its length and by how its elements are made (a checked type, a literal, <code>range</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>push</code>, a part of another list), so <code>all(xs, p)</code> is proven on an arbitrary element and, once known, gives <code>p</code> to every element the code looks at, a value of a union (<code>Square | Circle</code>) is a tag saying which member's shape it has plus a value for each member (a member value passed where the union is expected, or branches returning different members, become one; a <code>match</code> arm narrows it to its member), branches are merged with <code>ite</code>, and a loop forgets the variables it changes. Each check becomes the question \"can the negation be true under what is known here?\", which is sent to the solver (Z3, or cvc5) as SMT-LIB text (<code>verify --smt</code> prints the queries, termination ones included). <code>unsat</code> means proven; <code>sat</code> comes with a model, which becomes the counterexample. Anything not modelled (floats, dicts, sets, list contents, most higher-order builtins, unions whose members can share a shape such as <code>List[Int] | List[String]</code> or include a type parameter) becomes an unknown value. That can only make a proof fail, never make a false proof succeed, and the reason is kept for the report. <code>Int</code> is a mathematical integer, as it is when the code runs. Strings are sequences of characters (code points), as <code>chars()</code> counts them. These string builtins are modelled exactly: <code>len</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>starts_with</code>, <code>ends_with</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>chars</code> (its length), <code>index_of</code>, <code>replace</code> (every match, and a check that the pattern is not empty, which is a runtime fault), <code>to_string</code> of an <code>Int</code>, <code>Bool</code> or <code>String</code>, and <code>is_digit</code>. <code>parse_int</code> is exact for digits with an optional <code>-</code>, and for strings without a digit (<code>None</code>). Other forms (whitespace, <code>+</code>, <code>_</code>) are unknown. <code>trim</code> keeps only a length bound. <code>to_upper</code>, <code>to_lower</code>, <code>is_alpha</code>, <code>ord</code>, <code>chr</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>lines</code>, <code>repeat</code> and <code>reverse</code> are unknown. <code>matches(s, \"regex\")</code> becomes a regular-expression constraint with the interpreter's meaning. Strata regexes are Unicode regexes: <code>\\d</code> is any decimal digit, <code>\\w</code> and <code>\\s</code> are Unicode classes, <code>\\p{..}</code> works, <code>.</code> is any character but <code>\\n</code>, and <code>(?i)</code> is Unicode case folding. The pattern is parsed the way the interpreter parses it, and translated exactly: literals, classes, groups, alternation, repetition, and <code>^</code> / <code>$</code> at the very start or end. Word boundaries, multi-line anchors and anchors elsewhere stay unknown. Z3 can't decide classes with hundreds of ranges (<code>\\w</code>, <code>\\p{L}</code>, Unicode <code>\\d</code>), so such a class is bounded from both sides: <code>matches</code> is true on a subset of its ranges and false outside a superset of them. When one string is matched against two patterns, how their languages relate (one inside the other, or disjoint) is worked out exactly from the patterns' automata and added as a fact. So <code>\\d{3}</code> implies <code>\\d+</code>, and <code>[a-z]+</code> implies <code>\\w+</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Strings past U+2FFFF.</strong> SMT-LIB strings hold the code points U+0000 to U+2FFFF (Z3 and cvc5 both stop there). Rust strings go up to U+10FFFF. So a proof about strings covers the strings whose characters are all at most U+2FFFF. A string literal with a later character (planes 3 to 16: rare CJK, tags, private use) is an unknown. The cross-check, which runs proven functions on generated inputs, is the backstop.</p>\n<p><strong>Helpers in contracts.</strong> A contract can use an ordinary function, such as <code>type Digit = Int where small(it)</code> with <code>fn small(n: Int) -&gt; Bool { 0 &lt;= n &amp;&amp; n &lt; 10 }</code>. Inside a contract, a helper that terminates and cannot fail is <em>seen through</em>: its body is used, not just its contract. So <code>fn zero() -&gt; Digit { 0 }</code> is proven and <code>fn ten() -&gt; Digit { 10 }</code> is refuted. A helper that may not terminate is never seen through. Unfolding one would let the solver prove false things. A helper marked <code>terminates</code> is seen through, and the proof says it relied on that.</p>\n<p><strong>Termination.</strong> <code>verify</code> also says whether each function always finishes:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>terminates</strong>, when shown automatically:</li><li>no recursion, and only <code>for</code> loops;</li><li>a <code>while a &lt; b</code> loop (also <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>!=</code>) whose bound <code>b - a</code> the solver proves goes down on every pass, unless the pass ends the loop;</li><li>recursion where some argument is a smaller piece of a parameter, taken apart by a <code>match</code> (<code>[_, ..rest] =&gt; 1 + length(rest)</code>);</li><li>recursion with a <em>measure</em>: an integer expression of the parameters that the solver proves is never negative at each recursive call, and smaller for the call's arguments. The candidates are tried automatically:</li><li>each integer parameter (<code>fact(n - 1)</code>, <code>gcd(b, a % b)</code>);</li><li>the length of each list or string parameter (<code>total(drop(xs, 1))</code>);</li><li>for each comparison in the body, the distance between its sides (<code>100 - n</code> in <code>up(n + 1)</code> under <code>n &lt;= 100</code>, <code>hi - lo</code> in a binary search);</li><li>pairs of these compared in order, like words in a dictionary (<code>(m, n)</code> for Ackermann's function).</li></ul>\n<p>  It also needs everything the function calls to terminate.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>may not terminate</strong>, with the reason: a <code>loop</code>, other recursion, mutual recursion, or a call to such a function. The viewer marks these.</li><li><strong>assumed</strong>: the function is marked <code>terminates</code> (after its contract, <code>fn f(x: Int) -&gt; Int terminates { … }</code>). Its callers say they terminate <em>if</em> it does.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Policies.</strong> A package's <code>strata.toml</code> says what <code>verify</code> requires, each rule as a list of modules (<code>\"*\"</code> for all of them, <code>\"main\"</code> for the root module; a module covers the modules below it):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"toml\">[verify]\nproven = [\"core\"]        # every check proven, not just fuzzed\nterminates = [\"*\"]       # every function shown (or marked) to terminate\nno_recursion = [\"core\"]  # no recursion at all, even recursion that terminates</pre>\n<p>Recursion is allowed unless <code>no_recursion</code> says otherwise. A run that breaks a rule fails, listing each violation. <code>proven</code> accepts assumed code, and the run lists everything it accepted that way. <code>verify --require proven,terminates,no-recursion</code> applies the same rules to everything checked, package or not. <code>strata ci</code> rejects a rule that names a module the package doesn't have.</p>\n<p><strong>Cross-check.</strong> A proven function that terminates is still run on a few generated inputs (<code>--cross-check N</code>, 20 by default, 0 turns it off). If one of them fails, the prover was wrong, and <code>verify</code> says so: the result is a failure <em>found by the cross-check</em>, flagged as a bug in the prover. Each run has a step budget. A run that goes past it, or past the recursion limit, proves nothing either way and is set aside: proven code can still be far too costly to run, like Ackermann's function.</p>\n<p><strong>Solvers.</strong> <code>z3</code> is found on the <code>PATH</code>. <code>STRATA_SOLVER</code> names another solver binary, for example <code>STRATA_SOLVER=cvc5</code>. A solver whose <code>--version</code> names cvc5 is run with cvc5's flags; any other is run like Z3. Both get the same SMT-LIB queries. Without a solver, everything is fuzzed. Each query has a deterministic resource limit rather than a timeout (<code>rlimit</code> for Z3, <code>--rlimit-per</code> for cvc5, with a 20-second time limit only as a backstop), so results can be cached by content hash. The solver's version is in the cache key. The test suite passes with either solver. cvc5 has no Homebrew formula; install it with <code>brew tap cvc5/cvc5 &amp;&amp; brew install cvc5</code>, or from its releases.</p>","toc":[{"n":2,"id":"refinements","text":"Refinements"},{"n":2,"id":"recursion-and-termination","text":"Recursion and termination"},{"n":2,"id":"loops","text":"Loops"},{"n":2,"id":"lists","text":"Lists"},{"n":2,"id":"bugs-the-solver-finds","text":"Bugs the solver finds"},{"n":2,"id":"reference","text":"Reference"}]},"tools":{"title":"Tools","html":"<p>The command line, the viewer, and how a codebase is stored and merged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-tools-tell-you\">What the tools tell you</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">$ strata check                               # static errors are caught before running\nerror in status: non-exhaustive match on `Order`: `Cancelled` is not covered\nerror in sneaky: calling `load`: `String` does not implement `Db`\nerror in bad_qty: refinement violated at compile time in field `Line.qty`: 120 does not satisfy `Quantity` (where 1 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 99)\n\n$ strata holes\n?refund_policy  in refund\n  goal:    Cents  (= Int + refinement)\n  context: o: Order\n  must:    ensures it &gt;= 0\n\n$ strata verify                              # prove every contract with Z3; fuzz what it can't prove\n✓ line_total               proven (2 checks)\n✓ total                    proven (3 checks)\n✓ discount                 proven (2 checks)\n✓ ship                     proven (5 checks)\n○ refund                   incomplete: reached unfilled hole `?refund_policy` (via refund)\n\n$ strata verify bump                         # fn bump(q: Quantity) -&gt; Quantity { q + 1 }\n✗ bump                     Refinement violation (found by the solver)\n    input:   [99]\n    result of `bump`: 100 does not satisfy `Quantity` (where 1 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 99) (stack: bump)\n\n$ strata edit -m \"steeper discount\" &lt; discount.strata\nfn     discount                 changed  #fc40aad7… → #2e54e58d…  (implementation only — dependents unaffected)\n\n$ strata edit &lt; breaking_change.strata\nerror in test \"totals\": call to `total`, argument 1: expected `Int`, found `Line`\nrejected: the codebase would not check; nothing was changed\n\n$ strata rename total order_total\nrenamed total → order_total\nupdated 1 definition that refers to it: test \"totals\"\n\n$ strata rename Shipped Sent              # a variant; trait methods work the same way\nrenamed Shipped → Sent\nupdated 3 definitions that refer to it: order_id, ship, test \"shipping a pending order saves it as shipped\"\n\n$ strata rename Line.qty quantity         # a field: accesses, literals, updates, patterns\nrenamed Line.qty → Line.quantity\nupdated 3 definitions that refer to it: line_total, test \"shipping a pending order saves it as shipped\", test \"totals\"\n\n$ strata rename A.tag label B.tag label   # several renames in one step (here, a field read from `A | B`)\nrenamed A.tag → A.label, B.tag → B.label\nupdated 1 definition that refers to them: tag_of\n\n$ strata move charge billing              # to another module (file): references and imports follow\nmoved orders.charge from orders to billing (billing.strata)\nupdated 1 definition that refers to it: test \"charges\"\nimports: orders: use billing.charge; main: use billing.charge\n\n$ echo 'fn tax(amount: Int, rate: Int) -&gt; Int { amount * rate / 100 }' | strata edit -m \"tax takes a rate\"\nthe edit breaks 2 uses elsewhere; they are now holes, in a pending change:\n  ?pending_1  in total\n      was: tax(amount)\n      why: call to `tax`: expects 2 arguments, got 1\n  ?pending_2  in double_tax\n      was: tax(a)\n      why: call to `tax`: expects 2 arguments, got 1\nfill them with `strata edit`, then `strata done` (or `strata abort` to undo the whole change)\n\n$ strata done                             # once the holes are filled: one log entry for it all\nrecorded \"tax takes a rate\" as one change: 3 definitions\n\n$ strata edit total                       # nothing piped in: opens `total` in $VISUAL / $EDITOR;\n                                          # what you save is the edit\n$ strata edit shop                        # the module `shop` (or `shop.strata`, also a new module)\n$ strata edit main.main                   # the definition `main` of the root module `main`\n$ strata edit                             # the whole package, one file per module\n$ strata edit shop &lt; defs.strata          # piped: the definitions go into the module `shop`\n\n$ strata run total '[[{\"sku\":\"tea\",\"qty\":2,\"unit_price\":450},{\"sku\":\"cup\",\"qty\":1,\"unit_price\":1200}]]' --trace\n{\"event\":\"call\",\"fn\":\"total\",\"args\":[[{\"sku\":\"tea\",\"qty\":2,\"unit_price\":450},{\"sku\":\"cup\",\"qty\":1,\"unit_price\":1200}]],\"depth\":1}\n{\"event\":\"call\",\"fn\":\"line_total\",\"args\":[{\"sku\":\"tea\",\"qty\":2,\"unit_price\":450}],\"depth\":2}\n{\"event\":\"return\",\"fn\":\"line_total\",\"value\":900,\"depth\":2}\n{\"event\":\"call\",\"fn\":\"line_total\",\"args\":[{\"sku\":\"cup\",\"qty\":1,\"unit_price\":1200}],\"depth\":2}\n{\"event\":\"return\",\"fn\":\"line_total\",\"value\":1200,\"depth\":2}\n{\"event\":\"return\",\"fn\":\"total\",\"value\":2100,\"depth\":1}\n2100\n\n$ strata run total '[[]]'                    # arguments are checked against the types\nerror: invalid input: lines: [] does not satisfy `NonEmpty` (where len(it) &gt; 0)</pre>\n<p>Outside a package, <code>strata --file F edit &lt; text</code> writes a script (or a folder's module) as given, then checks it: what doesn't check is still written, its errors are printed with <code>written, but F doesn't check</code>, and the exit code is 1. (A package's <code>strata edit</code> refuses such an edit instead.) One module file of a folder isn't checked alone, since its neighbours may not be written yet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compiling-to-native-code\">Compiling to native code</h2>\n<p><code>strata build</code> compiles a program to an executable through LLVM: it writes LLVM IR as text, and <code>clang</code> compiles it with a small C runtime (<code>STRATA_CC</code> chooses another compiler). <code>--emit-llvm</code> prints the IR instead.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">$ strata build tests/data/build/numbers.strata -o numbers\nbuilt numbers\n$ ./numbers\n2432902008176640000\ngcd = 6, 2^10 = 1024, A(2, 3) = 9\n...\nfault (Refinement): result of `next_digit`: 10 does not satisfy `Digit` (where 0 &lt;= it &amp;&amp; it &lt;= 9)</pre>\n<p>A compiled program behaves as <code>strata run</code>: the same output, and contracts are checked at run time with the same fault messages (refinements of parameters, results, annotated bindings, declared variables and new values, <code>requires</code>, <code>ensures</code>, <code>assert</code>), exiting with 101. Only part of the language compiles so far:</p>\n<ul><li><code>Int</code>, which never overflows, as the interpreter's: one in [-2^62, 2^62) is a machine integer, and arithmetic on it stays inline; any other is a big integer in the runtime (kept in the 64-bit values outside that range, so comparing a small and a big integer needs no call). <code>Float</code>, <code>Bool</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>()</code>, and refinements of them;</li><li>records, variants (<code>Option</code>, <code>Result</code> and your own), tuples, <code>distinct</code> types, unions of variants; field access, <code>{ ..e, f: x }</code>, assigning a field (<code>x.a.b = e</code>), <code>is</code>, <code>==</code> on any of them, and printing them as the interpreter does;</li><li>generic functions and types (each compiled once per set of type arguments), a type's own methods (<code>impl Item { ... }</code>), and traits: implementations chosen from the type where the call is compiled (default methods, supertraits, generic traits and implementations, static methods like <code>T.empty()</code>, a variant's own implementation chosen by its tag), with the trait's <code>requires</code> and <code>ensures</code> on every implementation;</li><li>lists: literals, <code>+</code>, list patterns (<code>[x, ..rest]</code>), <code>for</code> over a list, <code>xs[i]</code> and slices (<code>xs[1..]</code>), and the list built-ins (<code>len</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>push</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>drop</code>, <code>range</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>fold</code>, <code>all</code>, <code>any</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>reverse</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>sort_by</code>, <code>zip</code>, <code>enumerate</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>join</code>), most of them written in Strata (<code>src/llvm/prelude.strata</code>);</li><li>dictionaries and sets: literals (<code>Dict { \"a\": 1 }</code>, <code>Set { 1, 2 }</code>, typed empty ones), <code>dict</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>items</code>, <code>map_values</code>, <code>to_list</code>, <code>union</code>, <code>intersection</code>, <code>difference</code>, <code>len</code>, <code>for</code> over them (a dictionary gives <code>(key, value)</code> pairs), <code>==</code>, printing (<code>Debug</code> / <code>Display</code>), and the refinements of their elements, keys and values. Each is a list kept in the interpreter's order (a set's elements, a dictionary's pairs by key), so equal collections are equal lists; their built-ins are Strata over lists, given the order of the element type;</li><li>strings: <code>+</code>, comparisons, <code>s[i]</code> and slices (<code>s[1..]</code>), and the string built-ins (<code>len</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>lines</code>, <code>trim</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>starts_with</code>, <code>ends_with</code>, <code>index_of</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>repeat</code>, <code>reverse</code>, <code>chars</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>to_upper</code>, <code>to_lower</code>, <code>is_digit</code>, <code>is_alpha</code>, <code>is_space</code>, <code>ord</code>, <code>chr</code>, <code>parse_int</code>, <code>parse_float</code>, <code>to_json</code>), indexed by character as the interpreter does; case mappings and <code>is_alpha</code> use tables made from Rust's, which the interpreter uses;</li><li>lambdas and closures, named functions as values (<code>filter(is_prime)</code>), functions returning functions;</li><li>functions and recursion, bindings (<code>x := e</code>, with patterns, <code>let x: T = e</code>), <code>mut</code> variables and assignment (<code>=</code>, <code>+=</code>, …), <code>if</code>, <code>match</code> with every pattern, <code>while</code>, <code>loop</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, <code>return</code>, <code>assert</code>, <code>!</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>except</code>;</li><li>f-strings with format specs (<code>{x:.2}</code>, <code>{n:&gt;5}</code>, <code>{n:05}</code>, <code>{s:*^9}</code>), and <code>print</code>, <code>to_string</code>, <code>abs</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>floor</code>, <code>ceil</code>, <code>round</code>, <code>sqrt</code>;</li><li><code>mut</code> parameters and receivers (<code>restock(mut line.qty, 5)</code>, <code>mut console.print(s)</code>): the function changes the caller's variable in place, and the variable's declared type is checked after the call;</li><li>capabilities: <code>main</code> can ask for <code>Console</code> (std.io) and capabilities such as <code>Disk</code> and <code>Process</code> (std.fs, std.process), and gets the terminal, the disk and the process, as with <code>strata run</code> (the program's arguments too). A parameter whose type is a trait (<code>console: Console</code>) is compiled once per implementation passed to it, as the language defines it (<code>console: C</code> for some <code>C: Console</code>): the real console and a <code>Recorder</code> in a test each get their own. A trait used as the type of a field, a list element or a <code>let</code> (<code>{ console: Console }</code>, <code>List[Shape]</code>) holds any implementation: the value carries its implementation's table of methods, and calls go through it;</li><li>options: <code>main</code> can ask for a record that derives <code>CommandLine</code> (std.cli); the compiled program reads it with the same <code>from_args</code>, so <code>--help</code> and the messages for bad arguments are <code>strata run</code>'s (exit code 0 after the help, 2 after an error). The usage line names the executable as it was started (<code>strata build</code> names it after the package, as <code>strata run</code> does);</li><li><code>main</code> returns <code>()</code>, an exit code, or a <code>Result</code> of either.</li></ul>\n<p>A fault prints the call stack as <code>strata run</code> does (<code>    stack: main -&gt; inner -&gt; half</code>), and running out of stack is a fault (<code>fault (Limit): stack overflow (recursion too deep)</code>), not a crash. There is no other limit: loops run as long as they run, and a recursion as deep as the stack allows (the interpreter too); the <code>stack:</code> line shows a recursion as one entry (<code>main -&gt; down (95846 times)</code>).</p>\n<p><code>matches(s, re)</code> with a literal pattern is compiled to a DFA when the program is (the same engine as the interpreter's, so the same strings match). Anything else (a pattern built at run time, for one) is refused, naming the function and what it uses: `<code>can't compile </code>main<code>: </code>matches<code> with a pattern that isn't a literal is not compiled yet</code><code>. Memory is reference-counted: a value is freed as soon as nothing holds it. With </code>STRATA_MEMORY<code> set, a compiled program reports how many objects it allocated and freed when it exits (</code>memory: 128 allocated, 128 freed, 0 live<code>); with </code>STRATA_MEMORY=check<code> it also keeps freed memory aside, stops on any use of a freed object (</code>memory: drop of a freed object<code>), and lists the objects still alive at the end (the tests of </code>strata build` run every program this way).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-jit\">The JIT</h3>\n<p><code>strata verify</code> runs only compiled code (<code>--jit</code> says why something can't be compiled yet, which is then skipped). Fuzzing: a driver per function generates its arguments (the generators <code>mock</code> uses), records them for a counterexample, and calls it, all in one module compiled once; a trait method of an implementation, and a law, are fuzzed through a wrapper function (a law's ensures it holds). Loops and calls count steps, so a runaway case is a <code>Limit</code> fault (<code>too many steps</code>). The solver's counterexamples are confirmed by running them compiled (a probe binding the values to the parameters' types, then calling the function), and so are literal arguments that break a refinement (<code>attr(\"onclick\", …)</code>), an error before anything runs. Probes, like compile-time code, are compiled without optimization: they run once.</p>\n<p><code>strata test</code> compiles the tests the same way: each <code>test</code> block is a function, a fault ends the test instead of the process, and what a test prints is kept for the report (<code>strata test --emit-llvm</code> prints the tests' IR). A test that can't be compiled yet fails, saying why; the others run. <code>--interpret</code> runs them in the old interpreter.</p>\n<p><code>strata run</code> compiles the same IR in memory with LLVM's libraries and runs it (the JIT), so a program runs at native speed without a build step (<code>fib(32)</code>: 26 ms, compilation included). What can't be compiled yet is an error that says why; nothing is interpreted instead. <code>strata run f [JSON-ARGS]</code> calls one function, compiled (its arguments written into a function that calls it, its result read back and printed as JSON), and <code>--trace</code> has compiled code print each call and return. A pattern built at run time (<code>matches(s, p)</code>, <code>p</code> not a literal) is matched by Rust's <code>regex</code>, which strata gives the runtime (a program built with <code>strata build</code> has only literal patterns). <code>strata run --interpret</code> runs the old interpreter, kept until it is removed to compare compiled code with (the build tests do). Compile-time code runs on the JIT too: in a <code>derive</code>, and <code>comptime e</code> anywhere else, whose value is computed while compiling and written into the program as a constant (<code>re := comptime parse(\"[a-z]+\")</code> never calls <code>parse</code> when the program runs). <code>e</code> may use definitions, not the variables around it.</p>\n<p>LLVM 23's libraries are linked into strata, so building it needs them (nothing is needed to run it):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sh\">brew install llvm                      # or apt.llvm.org's llvm-23-dev\nLLVM_SYS_231_PREFIX=$(brew --prefix llvm) cargo build --release</pre>\n<p>Both ways share one code generator (the IR as text, which LLVM parses for the JIT) and one runtime (<code>runtime.c</code>, built into <code>strata</code> for the JIT, compiled with the program by <code>strata build</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"serving-the-web\">Serving the web</h2>\n<p><code>strata serve [PATH] [--port N]</code> (default port 8000) runs a program as a web server: each request is turned into a <code>std.http.Request</code> and passed to the program's <code>fn handle(req: Request) -&gt; Response</code>. Like <code>main</code>, <code>handle</code> may also ask for <code>Console</code> and capabilities (<code>Disk</code>, <code>Process</code>, the program's own). Requests are handled one at a time, each with a fresh interpreter. A runtime fault, or a response the server can't send (a header holding a line break), becomes a 500 that says why, and every request is logged:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">$ strata serve examples/page\nServing on http://127.0.0.1:8000  (Ctrl-C to stop)\nGET / 200 (17 ms)\nGET /languages/haskell 200 (0 ms)\nPOST / 405 (0 ms)</pre>\n<p><strong>Apps.</strong> A program with no <code>handle</code> but with <code>init</code>, <code>update</code> and <code>view</code> is served as an app (the Elm architecture): <code>fn init() -&gt; Model</code>, <code>fn update(model: Model, msg: Msg) -&gt; Model</code>, <code>fn view(model: Model) -&gt; Html[Msg]</code>. Each page load starts a session whose model the server keeps in memory; the page loads a small script (<code>/_strata/app.js</code>, built into <code>strata</code>) that sends the message of the element the user clicks (<code>on_click(msg)</code>; also <code>dblclick</code>, <code>submit</code>, <code>change</code>), one at a time, and puts the new view in place. Handlers that make a message from what the user entered (<code>on_input</code>, <code>on_change</code>, <code>on_submit_form</code>) can't be written into the page: <code>render</code> numbers them, the page sends the number with the value or the form's fields, and the server calls <code>std.web.dispatch</code> on the current view to make the message. The page is patched rather than replaced, so focus and scroll stay, and the field being typed in keeps what was typed (after a submit, the view's value wins, so a form can clear it). A message is read against <code>Msg</code>, refinements included, so a forged one is refused (400); a fault in <code>update</code> is a 500 shown at the bottom of the page, and the model stays as it was; a session the server no longer has (it restarted) reloads the page. See <code>examples/counter</code>, and <code>examples/todos</code> for a form.</p>\n<p><code>handle</code> is a plain function, so tests call it directly (<code>handle(get_request(\"/\"))</code>). See <code>examples/page</code> and, for routes, <code>std.http.Router</code> in <a href=\"../docs/language.md\">the language</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-viewer\">The viewer</h2>\n<p><img alt=\"The Strata viewer, showing collatz_steps from examples/math.strata: its code, six proven checks, and \"may not terminate\"\" src=\"../docs/images/viewer.png\"></p>\n<p><em><code>strata ui</code> on <a href=\"../docs/../examples/math.strata\"><code>examples/math.strata</code></a> after <code>strata verify</code>. Left: every definition, with a dot for its status. Middle: the selected function and what surrounds it. Right: its description, neighbours, and what the verifier said: six checks proven, termination not shown.</em></p>\n<p><code>strata ui</code> serves a local, read-only viewer. It re-reads the codebase every 1.5 seconds, so edits made through the CLI (by you or by agents) appear as they are committed.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Code</strong>: with a definition selected (the default once you click something), it shows that definition, then how it takes part in implementations (the impl blocks of a type or a trait; an impl block's trait and type), its dependencies, its references and its tests, as plain code with a small kind/status marker in the margin. Each definition shows its interface; where there is more (a function body, an implementation's methods, a trait's default methods), an inline <em>show body ▸</em> / <em>hide body ▾</em> control reveals it. The selected definition starts open, and <em>expand all</em> opens everything. <em>Everything</em> shows the whole codebase.</li><li><strong>Hover a name</strong> in the code to get an arrow to where that definition is on screen (or which way to scroll); click it to go there.</li><li><strong>Inspector</strong> (right): the selected definition's name and description (its <code>///</code> doc comment, which is not repeated in the code), the traits it implements (or, for a trait, the types implementing it), a small graph of its neighbours (dependencies on the left, references on the right, all clickable), its holes and verification result with any counterexample, and its history.</li><li><strong>Type map</strong>: a graph of types and traits and how they depend on each other: <em>contains</em> (a field or variant mentions it), <em>refines</em>, <em>in a trait method</em> (a trait's method signatures mention it) and <em>extends</em> (supertraits). <em>implementations</em> adds the edges from each type to the traits it implements; <em>refinement types</em> adds types such as <code>type TaskId = Int where it &gt; 0</code>. Both are off by default. Each module is a region. The layout puts the most connected definitions at the centre of their module. Each card's code is highlighted as in the code view. Clicking a card shows it in the inspector; double-click opens it in the code view. Drag cards (positions are remembered), scroll to zoom, or <em>re-layout</em>.</li><li><strong>Activity</strong>: the patch log. Definitions changed since your last look are highlighted.</li><li><strong>Changes</strong> (key <code>d</code>): a structural diff. On a codebase it covers patches from the log (by default everything after the first import; pick another start), one step per patch. On files (<code>--file</code>) under git it compares a revision (default <code>HEAD</code>, or a branch or commit you type) with the working tree, one step per commit plus uncommitted changes. Click a step, or use ← →, to see only that step. Each changed definition says what happened in words (a variant added, a method added, a stronger guarantee, renamed) and shows its code with the changes marked in place: unchanged members and lines fold away, keeping the lines that enclose each change; a new <code>ensures</code> is bold with a <em>stronger guarantee</em> pill (a removed one: <em>weaker</em>, a new <code>requires</code>: <em>stricter precondition</em>); a mostly rewritten body falls back to a line diff; a doc change is a word diff. Each change shows only what it needs: a rename is one line (<code>Order.Shipped → Order.Sent</code>); a new signature or contract shows the signature and its conditions, not the body; a changed body is shown. Definitions that only follow an interface change (a rename, or call sites given a new argument) are listed under it, each opening to its own diff, and <em>show the code</em> opens the whole definition. Changes are ordered types → interfaces → implementations → tests, then verification before → after. The inspector shows what uses the selected definition, the steps that made the change, and its verification. <code>strata diff</code> prints the same thing as text (or <code>--json</code>).</li><li>The URL keeps the view, e.g. <code>#level=1&amp;sel=refund</code>, so you can send someone or an agent exactly what you're looking at.</li><li>Keys: <code>⌘P</code> / <code>Ctrl+P</code> opens a palette to jump to any definition by name (fuzzy, recent ones first), <code>c</code> code, <code>d</code> changes, <code>t</code> type map, <code>1</code> collapse all, <code>2</code> expand all, <code>/</code> filters, <code>Esc</code> clears the selection, <code>←</code> / <code>→</code> go back and forward through the definitions you have visited (the toolbar has the same buttons; in <em>Changes</em> they walk through the steps).</li></ul>\n<p><code>strata ui --file some.strata</code> views a single file instead of a codebase.</p>\n<h2 id=\"views-levels-of-detail\">Views (levels of detail)</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>--level 0</code></td><td>a map: one line per definition, with its dependencies, holes and verification status</td></tr><tr><td><code>--level 1</code></td><td>interfaces only: types, traits, signatures and contracts</td></tr><tr><td><code>--level 2</code></td><td>full source; <code>render</code> output parses back to the same tree</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Comments are part of the tree, so they survive every edit and render. A <code>//</code> comment belongs to what follows it: a definition (comments separated from it by a blank line, such as a file header or a section banner, keep that blank line), a field, a variant, a trait or impl method, a law, a statement or a match arm. Blank lines between statements are kept too (several in a row become one). A comment at the end of a line moves above the thing it follows. A comment anywhere else (inside an argument list, after the last statement of a block) is an error, never silently dropped. <code>///</code> doc comments work the same way and are the description the viewer shows. Comments are printed as written; changing one changes the definition's hash but not its interface hash, and does not invalidate verification results.</p>\n<p>All rendered code is formatted to about 80 columns: long argument lists, record literals, method chains, <code>&amp;&amp;</code> / <code>||</code> / <code>+</code> chains, parameter lists and doc comments are broken across lines.</p>\n<p>Selectors: <code>name</code>, <code>prefix*</code>, <code>slice:Name</code> (Name, what it uses, and what uses it), or a comma-separated combination. Every view is Strata text, so an agent can edit what it sees and send it back with <code>strata edit</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-is-stored\">How it is stored</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">strata.toml           the package: name, exports, dependencies, verifier policy, C to link ([native]),\n                      whether `main` may ask how it is run ([run] mode)\nmain.strata, a/b.strata, …\n                      one file per module, in canonical form (rewritten by `edit`, `add`, `rename`)\nstrata.log            one JSON entry per change made through strata: its id, message, which\n                      definitions changed (with their text before and after), interface vs.\n                      implementation, which dependents are affected, and renames\n.strata/verdicts.json local cache (not committed): verification results, keyed by the hash of a\n                      definition *and everything it depends on*</pre>\n<p>The files and <code>strata.log</code> are committed. The log is append-only and merges by union (<code>.gitattributes</code>), and each entry carries the definitions' text, so it replays without anything else: <code>strata diff</code> splits a git commit into the changes made through strata (with their renames), plus a step for edits made by hand. Because verification results are keyed by content, <code>verify</code> reruns only what is affected by a change.</p>\n<p>The code is stored as its syntax tree, in binary: each <code>.strata</code> file is a module file (the format is Strata's own, specified in <a href=\"../docs/binary-format.md\">binary-format.md</a>). Text goes in only through an edit (<code>strata edit</code>, <code>strata add</code>) and comes out through the views (<code>render</code>, <code>view</code>, <code>strata ui</code>); <code>strata render --split DIR</code> writes a package's text, one <code>.st</code> file per module, to read rather than to read back. Outside a package's log, <code>strata --file F edit &lt; text</code> stores a script (or, with <code>--file DIR/ edit MODULE</code>, a module of a folder), created if it is new; only text that doesn't parse is refused. Each module file starts with a header listing its definitions, their hashes and dependencies (<code>strata header core.strata</code> prints it), and each definition stores its interface before its bodies. <code>strata pack app.strata</code> writes the whole program as one bundle, which <code>--file</code>, <code>strata run</code> and every view read like the package. The hashes <code>strata</code> shows are those of the binary encoding (see the spec).</p>\n<h2 id=\"packages\">Packages</h2>\n<p>A folder with a <code>strata.toml</code> is a <strong>package</strong>. Its <code>.strata</code> files are its modules, named after their path inside it (<code>shop/orders.strata</code> is <code>shop.orders</code>). <code>main.strata</code> is the root module and the default entry point; a library has none. A folder inside a package that has its own <code>strata.toml</code> is a separate package. A <code>.strata</code> file outside every package is a standalone script (like <code>examples/bank.strata</code>).</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"toml\">name = \"tasks\"                         # `strata init` uses the folder's name; it stays when the folder moves (a worktree, a rename)\nexports = [\"core\", \"memory.MemQueue\"]  # optional: modules or definitions other packages can use; default every `pub`\n\n[deps]                                 # accepted, not fetched yet\njson = \"https://example.com/strata-json\"\n\n[verify]                               # what `strata verify` requires (see Verification)\nproven = [\"core\"]\n\n[json]                                 # how `std.json` reads JSON here\nunknown_keys = \"error\"                 # `decode` refuses keys a record doesn't have (default: \"ignore\")\nintegers = \"proven\"                    # integer fields must be proven to fit a JSON number (default: \"checked\", an error from `encode`)</pre>\n<p><code>strata ci</code> rejects an export that names nothing, or a definition that isn't <code>pub</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"git-merging-definition-by-definition\">Git: merging definition by definition</h2>\n<p>The <code>.strata</code> files are what you commit. They are binary, so <code>.gitattributes</code> marks them <code>binary diff=strata merge=strata</code>: with <code>strata setup-git</code>, <code>git diff</code> and <code>git log -p</code> show their text, and git merges them with the driver. GitHub shows no diff for them.</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>strata merge-file %O %A %B %P</code></strong> is a git merge driver. It pairs each side's definitions with the base's. A definition that only one side changed comes from that side, so edits to two functions never conflict, even when they sit on neighbouring lines. When both sides changed the same definition, its canonical text is merged line by line, and the result is kept only if it parses back into that one definition; otherwise conflict markers go around that definition alone, and the file is left as text: fix it, then <code>strata resolve FILE</code> stores it as a module file again. A rename on one side (a definition gone, and a new one that is the base's after <code>strata rename</code>) is applied to the other side, so new calls to the old name follow it. Then the file is type-checked inside its package: an error that neither side had on its own (ours changed a signature, theirs added a call to the old one) fails the merge. A side that isn't a module file isn't merged: ours stays.</li><li><strong><code>strata setup-git</code></strong> points this clone's git config at the driver and at <code>strata render --file</code> to show <code>.strata</code> files as text, and <code>.gitattributes</code> has <code>*.strata binary merge=strata diff=strata</code>, so <code>git merge</code>, <code>git pull</code>, <code>git diff</code> and <code>git log -p</code> use them.</li><li><strong>Every change is a commit.</strong> In a git repository, each change made through strata (an <code>edit</code>, <code>rename</code>, <code>move</code> or <code>add</code> that goes through, or a pending change at <code>strata done</code>) is committed with its message: <code>git log</code> is the history of changes, you can go back to any of them, and branches work as usual. Only the package's files are committed; whatever else is staged is left alone. Edits during a pending change aren't committed until it's done. Turn it off with <code>auto_commit = false</code> in <code>strata.toml</code>, or <code>--no-commit</code> on one command.</li><li><strong><code>strata sync [main]</code></strong> merges <code>origin/main</code> (or the local branch, without a remote) into the current branch with the driver, whether or not <code>setup-git</code> was run. Renames that either side made with <code>strata rename</code> (read from both sides' <code>strata.log</code>) are then applied to the other side's code, in every file: a call that main added to <code>area</code> becomes a call to <code>rect_area</code> if your branch renamed it, unless a local named <code>rect_area</code> would capture it. Then it runs <code>strata ci</code> and commits the merge only if that passes; otherwise the merge is left staged, so you can fix it or <code>git merge --abort</code>.</li><li><strong><code>strata ci [--fix]</code></strong> is what CI runs: every package and script in the repository checks, every package's exports exist, and every <code>.strata</code> file is in canonical form (exactly the bytes strata writes for it; <code>--fix</code> rewrites it). GitHub doesn't run merge drivers, so <code>.github/workflows/strata.yml</code> runs <code>strata ci</code> on the pull request's merge ref. Make it a required check, and require branches to be up to date before merging. Then a branch lands only once it was merged with <code>main</code> by <code>strata sync</code> and the result checks.</li></ul>","toc":[{"n":2,"id":"what-the-tools-tell-you","text":"What the tools tell you"},{"n":2,"id":"compiling-to-native-code","text":"Compiling to native code"},{"n":3,"id":"the-jit","text":"The JIT"},{"n":2,"id":"serving-the-web","text":"Serving the web"},{"n":2,"id":"the-viewer","text":"The viewer"},{"n":2,"id":"views-levels-of-detail","text":"Views (levels of detail)"},{"n":2,"id":"how-it-is-stored","text":"How it is stored"},{"n":2,"id":"packages","text":"Packages"},{"n":2,"id":"git-merging-definition-by-definition","text":"Git: merging definition by definition"}]}};
